      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fair enough — back to the science. Those hydrogen ions lower the pH inside the muscle cell, making it more acidic, and that acidity interferes with the proteins involved in muscle contraction. Specifically, it disrupts how calcium binds to troponin, which is the trigger for the actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling, and it also impairs the enzymes that regenerate ATP. Your nervous system picks up on this chemical chaos through group III and IV afferent nerves — specialized sensory neurons that run from the muscle to your spinal cord — which respond to chemical and metabolic stress. Those nerves send \"something's wrong in here\" signals up to your brain, which you experience as the burning sensation. So the burn is essentially a warning signal, not the damage itself.\n\nAnd the goblins — sorry, the group III and IV afferents — are basically the mine's alarm system.",
